The portable C as a program of 32 bits in the scenario start-memory: what it
is expected to show otherwise than the original. A patch of
harness/goldens/start-memory.txt, see harness/goldens.py: a line with - is the
golden's, a line with + is what is expected in its place.
Deviation: 1, 14 of src/portable_c/DEVIATIONS.md
Why: the second copy runs the command that was to leave it without memory for
its data, as the original's does, and is still there afterwards, where the
original's has said "Program too big to fit in memory" and ended: it keeps all
of itself above the megabyte, the memory of its panels and of the viewer too,
and needs none of DOS's to come back. The panels of the last three screens are
the copy's, with the mini status that the first copy's have not, and what the
command line shows of the copy is a row shorter. And the Advanced options have
no Quick execute commands: Clear keyboard buffer and what is below it in the
box stand a row further up, the cursor with them where it is on that box, the
box is a row shorter, and the two rows that its last row and the shadow below
it were on show what is behind the box, the upper one in the shadow

